WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.109
In this video, we'll learn about
routes across our round Earth and

00:00:04.109 --> 00:00:08.900
the coordinates behind them, and see how
they're used to plan the shortest flights.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.198
What's the shortest way
from London to Los Angeles,

00:00:13.198 --> 00:00:17.450
and why doesn't a plane fly
the way a flat map seems to point?

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.175
This is the sixth of seven
parts on coordinates.

00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:25.209
Now let's dive into the pieces
we need: scale factors,

00:00:25.209 --> 00:00:29.370
which turn a change in latitude
or longitude into kilometres,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.600
and the great circle, the shortest
route over our round Earth.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.748
Then let's meet the route that
sailors used for centuries,

00:00:37.748 --> 00:00:39.450
and see why they liked it.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.226
Let's start by putting our
coordinate systems side by side:

00:00:44.226 --> 00:00:46.382
polar coordinates on a flat map,

00:00:46.382 --> 00:00:51.975
cylindrical coordinates round a hurricane,
and latitude and longitude on the Earth.

00:00:52.325 --> 00:00:55.139
They're all called
curvilinear coordinates,

00:00:55.139 --> 00:00:58.150
which means coordinates
whose lines can curve.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.625
They share three features.

00:01:00.975 --> 00:01:05.521
First, the coordinate lines are
curves: the rings round a radar,

00:01:05.521 --> 00:01:08.575
the circles of latitude,
and the meridians.

00:01:08.925 --> 00:01:13.248
Second, the unit vectors change
from place to place: r hat and

00:01:13.248 --> 00:01:18.825
theta hat turn as we go round, and so do
the east and north arrows on the globe.

00:01:19.175 --> 00:01:23.725
Third, a change in a coordinate
isn't a distance by itself.

00:01:24.075 --> 00:01:27.913
Turning by one degree can
mean a short walk or a long one,

00:01:27.913 --> 00:01:29.700
depending on where you are.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:34.200
That third feature needs a name, so
let's look at it closely.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.475
How far do we walk when one
coordinate changes by a small step?

00:01:39.825 --> 00:01:44.307
In Part 1, we found the arc
rule: on a ring of radius r,

00:01:44.307 --> 00:01:49.350
turning by a small angle delta
theta walks r times delta theta.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.775
So for theta, the distance
walked is r times the step.

00:01:54.125 --> 00:01:58.543
That multiplier, the metres
walked per unit of the coordinate,

00:01:58.543 --> 00:02:00.325
is called a scale factor.

00:02:00.675 --> 00:02:03.625
For theta, the scale factor is r.

00:02:03.975 --> 00:02:06.709
On the Earth, as we found in Part 3,

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:10.126
stepping north by a small
change in latitude,

00:02:10.126 --> 00:02:14.075
delta phi, walks the Earth's
radius times delta phi.

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:18.000
So the scale factor of latitude
is the Earth's radius,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.053
which we write as the letter a,

00:02:20.053 --> 00:02:24.025
about six thousand three hundred
and seventy one kilometres.

00:02:24.375 --> 00:02:28.717
Stepping east by a small change
in longitude, delta lambda,

00:02:28.717 --> 00:02:30.410
walks round a parallel,

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:34.900
a circle whose radius is the Earth's
radius times cosine phi.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:40.825
So the scale factor of longitude
is the Earth's radius times cosine phi.

00:02:41.175 --> 00:02:45.950
Let's put numbers on it at Brest, at forty
eight point four degrees north.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:51.075
One degree is zero point zero
one seven four five radians.

00:02:51.425 --> 00:02:55.275
So one degree north walks
the Earth's radius times that,

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:58.575
about a hundred
and eleven point two kilometres.

00:03:00.075 --> 00:03:05.325
The cosine of forty eight point four
degrees is zero point six six four.

00:03:05.675 --> 00:03:07.742
So one degree east walks those

00:03:07.742 --> 00:03:11.668
hundred and eleven point two
kilometres times the cosine,

00:03:11.668 --> 00:03:14.975
only about seventy
three point eight kilometres.

00:03:16.475 --> 00:03:20.242
The same one-degree step
gives two different distances,

00:03:20.242 --> 00:03:23.050
and the scale factors keep track of that.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:28.449
Now let's put coordinates to work
on a question every airline asks:

00:03:28.449 --> 00:03:31.400
what's the shortest
way between two places?

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.800
It matters, because a shorter route
burns less fuel and takes less time.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:41.475
On a flat sheet of paper, the shortest
way is a straight line.

00:03:41.825 --> 00:03:44.683
But a plane can't tunnel
through the Earth,

00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:47.275
so it has to follow the curved surface.

00:03:47.625 --> 00:03:51.525
On a sphere, the shortest
way follows a great circle.

00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:57.400
A great circle is any circle on the sphere
whose centre is the centre of the Earth.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.761
The equator is one, and so
is every meridian,

00:04:00.761 --> 00:04:04.575
together with its partner
on the other side of the globe.

00:04:04.925 --> 00:04:08.319
The other circles of latitude
are not great circles:

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:10.800
their centres sit on the Earth's axis,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.325
above or below the Earth's
centre, so they're smaller.

00:04:15.125 --> 00:04:19.225
Let's find the great circle
through London and Los Angeles.

00:04:19.575 --> 00:04:25.050
London is at fifty one and a half degrees
north, almost on the prime meridian.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.464
Los Angeles is at thirty
four degrees north,

00:04:28.464 --> 00:04:31.250
and a hundred and eighteen degrees west.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.487
Picture a flat sheet slicing through the
globe, so that it passes through London,

00:04:36.487 --> 00:04:39.625
through Los Angeles,
and through the Earth's centre.

00:04:39.975 --> 00:04:44.318
Three points that aren't in a line
fix exactly one flat sheet,

00:04:44.318 --> 00:04:46.700
just as three legs steady a stool.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.218
Where the sheet cuts the Earth's
surface, it traces a great circle,

00:04:51.218 --> 00:04:55.075
and the part between the two
cities is the great circle route.

00:04:55.425 --> 00:04:57.425
Why is it the shortest?

00:04:57.775 --> 00:05:02.075
Think of all the circles on the globe
that pass through both cities.

00:05:02.425 --> 00:05:04.990
The great circle is the biggest of them,

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.875
so between the two cities
it bends the least.

00:05:08.225 --> 00:05:10.746
A string pulled tight between the two

00:05:10.746 --> 00:05:13.675
cities on a desk globe
lies right along it.

00:05:14.025 --> 00:05:19.336
And the puck from Part 4, gliding over
a smooth Earth with nothing pushing it,

00:05:19.336 --> 00:05:22.400
was moving along exactly
such a great circle.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.175
How long is this route?

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:29.675
Let's turn the globe, so that we
look straight at the slicing sheet.

00:05:30.025 --> 00:05:34.390
Now it's just a flat circle, with
the Earth's centre in the middle,

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.800
and its radius is the Earth's radius.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:42.350
Draw one arrow from the centre
to London, and another one to Los Angeles.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:47.675
The angle between these two arrows, at
the Earth's centre, is called sigma.

00:05:48.025 --> 00:05:52.400
The route is the arc of the circle
between the two arrow tips.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.961
And that's exactly the picture
of the arc rule from Part 1:

00:05:56.961 --> 00:06:01.100
the distance along a circle
is the radius times the angle.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:07.225
So the distance d equals the Earth's
radius times sigma, with sigma in radians.

00:06:08.725 --> 00:06:10.675
Here's a way to feel it.

00:06:11.025 --> 00:06:15.080
Every degree of sigma is one
degree of a great circle, about a

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.611
hundred and eleven point two
kilometres on the ground,

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:21.750
just like a degree
of latitude along a meridian.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:25.325
So once we know sigma, we
know the distance.

00:06:26.125 --> 00:06:30.950
The next few minutes work out sigma
step by step, with every number.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.741
They're here for anyone who
enjoys seeing every step,

00:06:34.741 --> 00:06:40.000
but you don't need them for the intuition
behind weather, climate science or GIS.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:44.241
If it feels like a lot, skip
ahead to the time on screen,

00:06:44.241 --> 00:06:46.425
and you won't miss the big idea.

00:06:46.775 --> 00:06:51.323
To find sigma, we need the two
position arrows of length one,

00:06:51.323 --> 00:06:55.125
r hat one for London
and r hat two for Los Angeles.

00:06:55.475 --> 00:07:02.175
From Part 3, each one is cosine phi cosine
lambda, cosine phi sine lambda, sine phi.

00:07:02.525 --> 00:07:06.333
London is at fifty
one point five one degrees north,

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:09.117
and zero point one three degrees west,

00:07:09.117 --> 00:07:12.925
so its lambda is minus
zero point one three degrees.

00:07:13.275 --> 00:07:18.836
The cosine of fifty one point five one
degrees is zero point six two two,

00:07:18.836 --> 00:07:24.550
and the cosine of minus zero point one
three degrees is almost exactly one.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.225
So the first part
is zero point six two two.

00:07:29.725 --> 00:07:33.182
The sine of minus
zero point one three degrees

00:07:33.182 --> 00:07:36.187
is minus zero point zero zero two three,

00:07:36.187 --> 00:07:39.869
so the second part, cosine
phi times sine lambda,

00:07:39.869 --> 00:07:42.875
is about minus zero point zero zero one.

00:07:44.375 --> 00:07:48.826
The third part is the sine of fifty
one point five one degrees,

00:07:48.826 --> 00:07:50.875
zero point seven eight three.

00:07:52.375 --> 00:07:56.832
Los Angeles is at thirty
four point zero five degrees north, and a

00:07:56.832 --> 00:08:00.141
hundred and eighteen point two four
degrees west,

00:08:00.141 --> 00:08:04.800
so its lambda is minus a hundred
and eighteen point two four degrees.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:08.329
Its cosine phi
is zero point eight two nine,

00:08:08.329 --> 00:08:12.304
its cosine lambda is minus
zero point four seven three,

00:08:12.304 --> 00:08:16.350
and its sine lambda is minus
zero point eight eight one.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:20.409
So its first part, cosine
phi times cosine lambda,

00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:23.450
is about minus zero point three nine two.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:28.465
Its second part, cosine
phi times sine lambda,

00:08:28.465 --> 00:08:31.750
is about
minus zero point seven three zero.

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:38.350
And its third part, the sine of thirty
four point zero five degrees,

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.450
is zero point five six zero.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:45.575
Now let's use the rule
from the dot product movie.

00:08:45.925 --> 00:08:50.044
The dot product of two arrows
is the product of their lengths,

00:08:50.044 --> 00:08:52.900
times the cosine
of the angle between them.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.770
Our two arrows both have length one,

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:59.200
so their dot product
is just the cosine of sigma.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.642
That's the shadow picture again:

00:09:01.642 --> 00:09:04.975
the shadow of one arrow
of length one on the other.

00:09:05.325 --> 00:09:06.906
To take the dot product,

00:09:06.906 --> 00:09:10.793
we multiply London's first
part by Los Angeles' first part,

00:09:10.793 --> 00:09:15.800
then the second parts, and then the third
parts, and add the three products.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:19.187
That gives about zero point one nine five,

00:09:19.187 --> 00:09:22.875
so the cosine of sigma
is zero point one nine five.

00:09:24.375 --> 00:09:28.450
Which angle has a cosine
of zero point one nine five?

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.175
A calculator's inverse cosine says
about seventy eight point seven degrees.

00:09:35.675 --> 00:09:38.544
A cosine just below
zero point two means an

00:09:38.544 --> 00:09:42.080
angle a little less than
ninety degrees, so London and

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:47.150
Los Angeles are almost a quarter of the
way round the Earth from each other.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.875
The arc rule needs sigma in radians.

00:09:51.225 --> 00:09:55.105
One degree is pi over a hundred
and eighty radians,

00:09:55.105 --> 00:09:58.300
about zero point zero one seven four five.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:03.501
So sigma, seventy eight point seven
three eight degrees to be exact,

00:10:03.501 --> 00:10:06.925
is about
one point three seven four two radians.

00:10:08.425 --> 00:10:11.175
Now the distance is the Earth's radius,

00:10:11.175 --> 00:10:16.675
six thousand three hundred and seventy
one kilometres, times sigma in radians.

00:10:17.025 --> 00:10:21.775
That's about eight thousand seven
hundred and fifty five kilometres.

00:10:23.275 --> 00:10:25.045
Let's check it the quick way:

00:10:25.045 --> 00:10:29.501
every degree of sigma is about a hundred
and eleven point two kilometres,

00:10:29.501 --> 00:10:32.004
so seventy eight point seven four degrees

00:10:32.004 --> 00:10:35.239
make about eight thousand
seven hundred and fifty six

00:10:35.239 --> 00:10:37.925
kilometres, the same
to within the rounding.

00:10:39.425 --> 00:10:43.442
At a cruising speed of about
nine hundred kilometres per hour,

00:10:43.442 --> 00:10:48.625
that's nearly ten hours in the air, before
any help or hindrance from the winds.

00:10:49.425 --> 00:10:52.100
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:54.996
Seen from the Earth's centre, London and

00:10:54.996 --> 00:10:58.115
Los Angeles are about
seventy nine degrees apart,

00:10:58.115 --> 00:11:01.106
so the great circle route
between them is about

00:11:01.106 --> 00:11:04.925
eight thousand seven hundred
and fifty five kilometres long.

00:11:05.275 --> 00:11:07.250
Where does this route go?

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.750
On most flat maps, Los Angeles looks
to the west-south-west of London.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.377
But the great circle leaves
London heading north-west,

00:11:16.377 --> 00:11:20.200
at a compass bearing of about
three hundred and twelve degrees.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:25.396
That heading comes from London's own
east and north arrows, from Part 4:

00:11:25.396 --> 00:11:30.310
we measure how much of Los Angeles'
position arrow points along each one,

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:32.800
and turn that into a compass bearing.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:38.354
It climbs to its highest latitude,
about sixty two point four degrees north,

00:11:38.354 --> 00:11:41.025
near the south-west coast of Greenland.

00:11:41.375 --> 00:11:44.118
Then it turns south over northern Canada,

00:11:44.118 --> 00:11:47.195
and arrives at Los Angeles
heading south-west,

00:11:47.195 --> 00:11:50.875
at a bearing of about two
hundred and fourteen degrees.

00:11:52.375 --> 00:11:57.725
So along the way, the compass heading
changes by almost a hundred degrees.

00:11:58.075 --> 00:12:03.475
That's why flights from Europe to
California pass over Greenland and Canada.

00:12:04.275 --> 00:12:07.525
Sailors used a different
route for centuries.

00:12:07.875 --> 00:12:09.625
It's called the rhumb line:

00:12:09.625 --> 00:12:13.254
a route that keeps the same
compass bearing all the way,

00:12:13.254 --> 00:12:16.300
so it crosses every
meridian at the same angle.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:20.920
From London to Los Angeles,
the rhumb line holds a bearing of about

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:25.700
two hundred and fifty eight and a half
degrees, a little south of due west.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:28.979
It stays further south
than the great circle,

00:12:28.979 --> 00:12:33.925
and it's about nine thousand seven
hundred and thirty three kilometres long.

00:12:35.425 --> 00:12:37.450
How much longer is that?

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.424
Dividing the rhumb line's length by the

00:12:40.424 --> 00:12:43.453
great circle's gives
about one point one one,

00:12:43.453 --> 00:12:46.750
so the rhumb line is about
eleven percent longer.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.227
The extra is about nine hundred
and seventy eight kilometres,

00:12:52.227 --> 00:12:56.400
more than an hour of flying at nine
hundred kilometres per hour.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.383
And if you kept following a rhumb line,

00:13:00.383 --> 00:13:03.375
it would spiral round
and round towards a pole.

00:13:04.175 --> 00:13:07.175
So why would anyone
choose the longer route?

00:13:07.525 --> 00:13:09.450
There are three reasons.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.650
The first is steering.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.625
A rhumb line needs one compass
setting for the whole trip:

00:13:15.625 --> 00:13:19.625
set two hundred and fifty eight
and a half degrees, and hold it.

00:13:19.975 --> 00:13:25.223
The great circle needs a new heading all
the time, from three hundred and twelve

00:13:25.223 --> 00:13:29.225
degrees at London to two hundred
and fourteen at Los Angeles.

00:13:29.575 --> 00:13:31.721
Before satellites and computers,

00:13:31.721 --> 00:13:36.147
a ship's navigator with a compass
could steer a rhumb line easily,

00:13:36.147 --> 00:13:39.500
but a great circle only
with a lot of calculation.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:45.275
The second reason is that, on short
trips, the extra distance is tiny.

00:13:45.625 --> 00:13:50.927
From Brest to Lisbon, about one thousand
one hundred and thirty eight kilometres,

00:13:50.927 --> 00:13:55.050
the rhumb line is only about a hundred
and fifty metres longer.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.775
The third reason is a map.

00:13:59.125 --> 00:14:04.750
On the most famous flat map, the Mercator
map, a rhumb line is a straight line.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.458
So a navigator could draw
the route with a ruler,

00:14:08.458 --> 00:14:10.925
read off its angle, and steer by it.

00:14:11.275 --> 00:14:14.638
That's why long voyages
were often split into a few

00:14:14.638 --> 00:14:18.134
rhumb-line legs that follow
the great circle closely:

00:14:18.134 --> 00:14:22.750
each leg is easy to steer, and the whole
route is nearly the shortest.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:28.870
To sum up, curvilinear coordinates have
curved lines, unit vectors that turn,

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:33.500
and scale factors that turn a change
of coordinate into a distance.

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:38.637
On the Earth, the scale factor
of latitude is the Earth's radius,

00:14:38.637 --> 00:14:42.025
and of longitude,
the radius times cosine phi.

00:14:42.375 --> 00:14:46.282
The shortest way between two
places follows a great circle,

00:14:46.282 --> 00:14:48.600
whose centre is the Earth's centre.

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:53.775
Its length is the Earth's radius
times sigma, the angle at the centre,

00:14:53.775 --> 00:14:58.600
and the cosine of sigma is the dot product
of the two position arrows.

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:04.008
From London to Los Angeles, that's
about seventy eight point seven degrees,

00:15:04.008 --> 00:15:08.325
or about eight thousand seven
hundred and fifty five kilometres.

00:15:08.675 --> 00:15:12.675
Along the great circle, the compass
heading changes all the way,

00:15:12.675 --> 00:15:16.800
from three hundred and twelve
degrees to two hundred and fourteen.

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:21.353
A rhumb line keeps one compass
bearing, so it's easy to steer,

00:15:21.353 --> 00:15:24.200
but here it's about eleven percent longer.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:27.838
In Part 7, we'll flatten
the Earth onto maps,

00:15:27.838 --> 00:15:31.125
and work out exactly
what each map stretches.

00:15:31.475 --> 00:15:36.541
That explains why the rhumb line comes out
straight on the most famous map of all,

00:15:36.541 --> 00:15:40.000
and why weather data on a grid
need care near the poles.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.958
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

00:15:44.958 --> 00:15:47.000
so watch out for the others.

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:51.747
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

00:15:51.747 --> 00:15:55.400
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

00:15:55.750 --> 00:16:00.578
The notebook for coordinates, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

00:16:00.578 --> 00:16:03.400
are on the website that
goes with this video.
